\title{latinobarometro: download and harmonize Latinobarometro survey waves}
\description{
Downloads waves of the Latinobarometro public opinion survey (1995-2024)
from the Latinobarometro Corporation archive, caches them, harmonizes
variable names across waves with the official time-series crosswalk, and
builds population-scaled weights for pooled regional analysis.
}
\section{Getting started}{

\preformatted{
lb_cache_dir(persist = TRUE)   # keep downloads between sessions (once)
d  <- lb_load(2023)            # one wave, harmonized names
p  <- lb_pool(c(2018, 2020, 2023), keep = c("X_001", "X_020", "P12ST_A"))
}
}

\section{Data terms}{

This package downloads survey data at run time and redistributes nothing.
Latinobarometro's terms of use prohibit republishing their data, which is
why there is deliberately no fallback mirror: if the archive is unreachable,
the package fails rather than serving a copy from elsewhere. Users are
responsible for complying with those terms and for citing the waves they
use, for example:

Latinobarometro Study 2023. Corporacion Latinobarometro: 2023 Wave -
Aggregated Version. Madrid: JD Systems Institute.
}

\section{Relationship to the Stata package}{

A companion Stata command, `latinobarometro`, implements the same pipeline.
Two differences are worth knowing. First, Stata needed elaborate repair of
variable and value-label-set names containing periods; R does not, because
periods are legal in R names and \pkg{haven} attaches value labels per
column instead of through shared named label sets. Second, waves 2013-2023
publish native R files, which this package prefers over the Stata `.dta`
archive unless you ask otherwise.
}

